            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the last word of the chapter: what did prāyaḥ mean, and what did kiñcit mean?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: बहु",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "बहु (bahu) — \"much, many\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "बहु (bahu) is \"much\" of one thing and \"many\" of several — the far end of the answer किञ्चित् (kiñcit) starts."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Greek pakhus, \"thick, stout\", is its cousin, and thickness is the sense underneath: plenty as bulk rather than as a count."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Hindi bahut, \"very, a lot\", is बहु with an ending on it, and it is one of the commonest words anyone says today."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Five replies about how much and how often: sometimes, suddenly, mostly, a little, much."
            }
          ]
        },
